            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

This house has just over 42' of vertical rise from the lower level entry to the top of the cathedral ceiling on the top floor. Tall houses have a lot of opportunity for "stack effect" heat loss. In your house, there is leakage around the lower level door into the rear unheated crawlspace, and through the upper level rear garden doors. There also appears to be air leakage around the 2 front doors and perhaps some coming through the Hi-Velocity fancoil ventilation system (located in the rear unheated crawlspace). In fact, you mentioned that your family does not use this ventilation system as the house appears to have adequate "natural air turnover", combined with the exhaust fans for the kitchen range hood fan &amp; two of the bathrooms.



For the Hi-Velocity ventilation system (with a hot water coil preheat), the unit itself and the distribution ductwork are all located in an unheated crawlspace. There is a "dampered" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air of this ventilation system - to mix in some fresh air &amp; provide make-up air when other exhaust fans are one.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

NOTE: For all energy savings calculations, the electric boiler is deemed to be the primary heating system, with the Pellet Boiler only used for backup. As I know you use the pellet boiler for 100% of your heating needs, I will display the energy savings associated with this upgrade recommendation as a percentage of your total estimated annual energy use for this house.



For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save 10%&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house have 6" (R24) of expanded polystyrene foam (EPS) insulation board insulation, and the wall that separates your house from the garage is 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. I have modeled the estimated annual energy savings if the wall adjoining the garage had an additional R20 and the rest of the walls with EPS had an additional R10 ......... in essence bringing all of the exposed walls up to the R32 - R34 range.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

NOTE: For all energy savings calculations, the electric boiler is deemed to be the primary heating system, with the Pellet Boiler only used for backup. As I know you use the pellet boiler for 100% of your heating needs, I will display the energy savings associated with this upgrade recommendation as a percentage of your total estimated annual energy use for this house.



Increasing the wall insulation by the amounts identified above is estimated to &lt;b&gt;save 6.7%&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>

There are a few locations where exposed floors exist in your home. The rear floor of your house (above the unheated crawlspace) was confirmed by you to contain 12" of pink fibreglass (R40). Two other exposed floor sections are on the 2nd floor along the right side that overhangs the garage and a small triangle section on the 3rd floor above the front entrance door. I have assumed both of these sections to be insulated with R40 as well.



The floor above the garage is an uninsulated slab of concrete with in-floor heating for the 2nd floor area above. As discussed, the garage area of your house is not considered as heated indoor living space for the purpose of the EnerGuide Rating Service. I therefore need to assess the insulation levels in the wall between the garage &amp; the indoor living space behind it (currently R12), as well as the exposed floor of the 2nd floor living space above (currently uninsulated). I have already commented in another area of this report about an R20 increase to the wall adjoining the garage. For the exposed floor above, I have modeled the addition of R30.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

NOTE: For all energy savings calculations, the electric boiler is deemed to be the primary heating system, with the Pellet Boiler only used for backup. As I know you use the pellet boiler for 100% of your heating needs, I will display the energy savings associated with this upgrade recommendation as a percentage of your total estimated annual energy use for this house.



By adding R30 to the floor above the garage, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save 10.25%/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 190 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</exposedFloors>
                <VentilationSystem>

HRV



Your house has a Lo-Velocity fancoil air circulation system, that is located in the unheated crawlspace section at the rear of your house. This ventilation system has a fresh air pipe (make up air for when an exhaust fan is operating) that can draw air from the crawlspace (controlled by a thumb damper on the pipe). There is also a pre-heat hot water coil to warm the air before being distributed throughout the house. Unfortunately, this system is located completed outside the heated living space of the home. You also mentioned that your house has enough natural air leakage (fresh air supply) that this Lo-Velocity fancoil is rarely used.



For exhaust only fans, your house has a downdraft fan for the kitchen range hood, two bathroom fans &amp; a clothes dryer. The make-up air for these comes from the air leakage locations we identified in your house, including the large amount of air coming from around the garage door (as you normally leave the interior door open between the house and the garage.



I would recommend that you research having a new ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. A new ENERGY STAR® rated HRV should be located inside the heated living area of your home, as well as the main supply &amp; return pipes to the HRV. The outside fresh air supply &amp; the exhaust air of the HRV should be located to the outside of that crawlspace ........... just like the exhaust pipe is for the kitchen range.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, the key savings would be associated with the reduced air leakage outlined elsewhere in my report, plus the benefits of reduced heat loss from the supply &amp; return pipes for the current Lo-Velocity unit no longer beingf located in an unheated section of the house. The pre-heat coil wiould also not have to work as hard. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



Your house currently has a 24 kW Super Hot electric boiler, that you supplement with your Pinnacle PB150 pellet boiler. For EnerGuide Rating Service purposes, the house is assessed assuming the electric boiler is the primary heating system and the biomass pellet boiler system is the supplemental system.



For your information, using the pellet boiler 100% of the time is estimated to reduce the electricity consumption of your house by 65.6%. Based on the current cost of electricity &amp; pellets in Yellowknife (outlined within this report), this 65.6% savings translates into $15,774 annually !!!



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system facing south, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,067&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range down draft fan, 2 bathroom fans, and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.1 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from your combustion appliances (the boiler). In fact, the pellet boiler is direct vent with an induced draft fan exhaust, so there is currently very little risk for the backdrafting of combustion gases.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



